oproliferative neoplasm (CML)?", "summary": "CML typically presents with splenomegaly; its absence slightly reduces the likelihood of this diagnosis, though other features like elevated LAP remain relevant."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the normal leukocyte differential (no lymphocytosis) contradict lymphoproliferative/plasma cell disorders as the primary explanation?", "summary": "The normal leukocyte differential reduces the likelihood of lymphoproliferative or plasma cell disorders, as these often present with specific cytopenias or blast counts."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of hypercalcemia without concurrent infection or inflammation contradict reactive leukocytosis as the primary explanation?", "summary": "Hypercalcemia without concurrent infection or inflammation weakly contradicts reactive leukocytosis, which typically has metabolic causes rather than calcium dysregulation."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B4", 0.379], ["B5", 0.329], ["B3", 0.292]]}, {"top": [["B5", 0.337], ["B3", 0.282], ["B4", 0.25]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No immediate life-threatening instability markers (airway, respiratory failure, shock, altered mental status, or uncontrolled bleeding). Hypercalcemia and leukocytosis are present but lack acute decompensation features requiring urgent intervention.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "No branch meets posterior ≥0.75 for confirmation. Dangerous alternatives B3 (CML) and B4 (plasma cell disorder) remain unresolved with posteriors 0.282 and 0.25 respectively. Further discriminators (e.g., bone marrow biopsy, lactate dehydrogenase, lytic bone lesions imaging) are needed to resolve uncertainty."}, "turn_budget_used": 2, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.7, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.33749661962520405, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic lymphoma"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chronic myeloid leukemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Leukemoid reaction"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Multiple myeloma"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'infection', 'inflammation', 'hypercalcemia', 'Leukocytosis'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B4", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.379}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B5", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "posterior": 0.337, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 1}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 1, "disconfirming": 1, "last_disconfirming_digest": "Hypercalcemia without concurrent infection or inflammation weakly contradicts reactive leukocytosis, which typically ha…"}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "refute"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B3", "B4", "B1"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=67%, source=both]\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, decreased circulating antibody level\n  Favours second: abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Metastatic Malignancy with Hypercalcemia:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, decreased circulating antibody level, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Metastatic Malignancy with Hypercalcemia:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, arterial thrombosis, ascites\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nChronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm vs Metastatic Malignancy with Hypercalcemia:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia, anti-thyroglobulin antibody positivity\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal obesity, abnormal cardiac septum morphology, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology\n\nRelated to Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: cd4+/cd56+ hematodermic neoplasm, hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, mast cell neoplasm\n\nRelated to Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, lymphoid leukemia (disease), lymphoma\n\nRelated to Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm: bone marrow cancer, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, not otherwise specified\n\nRelated to Metastatic Malignancy with Hypercalcemia: hypercalcemia disease, paraneoplastic syndrome\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue → fatigue → Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts\n  A 59-year-old man presents to his primary care physician for fatigue → fatigue → Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts"}
